# Observer card experiment

The **observer card experiment** is the minimal design proposed in the
discussion for testing whether an executable document can do more than feed
back on itself. One card links to itself, a cell in it retrieves a pinned
revision of that same card, and what the cell reports may lead to a reviewed
change of the card or of the rules that interpret it. The name and the design
come from the discussion and are not established outside it.

## Description

### The design

1. A card named `observer` references itself.
2. A cell in the card inspects a **pinned revision** of the card.
3. Evaluation emits structured observations.
4. A human or agent proposes an edit, either to the source or to its
   interpretive rules.
5. Git records the accepted revision together with the provenance of the
   output.

Two notations are involved, and the experiment depends on their difference.

```text
[[observer]]     a link: the card mentions itself
@[[observer]]    a retrieval: the card is fetched as a value a cell can inspect
```

The first is [[self-reference]] at the level of prose. The second turns the
card into data for a computation that lives inside the card. Going from the
one to the other is the step from a document that names itself to a document
that examines itself. The discussion showed only the bare retrieval and
declined to invent an analysis interface that HQL does not have.

### What it would and would not show

Steps one to three alone establish very little. A card that reads its own
text is in the position of a [[quine]]: it reproduces a description without
saying anything about it. Steps four and five add a [[feedback-loop]].

The experiment is aimed at step four's second option. If the observations
lead to a change in the interpretive rules, and those rules are what produced
the observations, then the card has acted on the mechanism that reads it.
That is the [[level-crossing]] the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] lacks.

### Execution boundaries

A card that reads and may rewrite itself needs limits. The discussion lists
five, and they are part of the experiment and not an afterthought.

- **Fixed snapshot.** The source revision and the dependency snapshot do not
  change during an evaluation. The cell reads a pinned revision, never the
  text being written.
- **No silent self-execution.** Generated text or code does not run merely
  because it was generated.
- **Changes as patches.** Edits by a human or an agent enter as revisions
  that can be reviewed.
- **Provenance.** Each output records the cell, the input commit, the
  dependencies and the runtime that produced it.
- **Termination.** Cycles need an explicit policy for evaluation and for
  stopping.

The pinned revision does the same work as the separation of
[[object-and-meta-level]] in logic. The card under inspection and the card
doing the inspecting are held apart as two revisions, so that the crossing
between them is a deliberate, recorded act.

### The formal cousin

The discussion also sketched a stricter experiment: implement syntax trees,
an encoding, substitution, proof checking and diagonalization in HQL. It came
with a warning that applies here too. Executing a construction from outside
is one thing, and proving that a theory represents the construction
internally is another. An observer card that runs is evidence of the first
only.

## Relations

- It is the smallest instance of the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: instance-of}.
- It starts from [[self-reference]] {relation: uses}, a card that links to itself.
- Its target is a [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses} between a card and the rules that read it.
- Without step four it is no more than a [[quine]] {relation: distinguished-from}: reproduction without comment.
- Its pinned revision keeps apart [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}.
- Its discipline is spelled out further in [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: uses}.
- Its proposals are made by a [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} or a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: uses}.
- Its stricter cousin would reproduce [[godel-numbering]] {relation: analogous-to} and the [[diagonal-lemma]] {relation: analogous-to} in HQL.
- It is the first concrete step toward [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[hmd-feedback-loop]].** Card, cell, output, interpretation, revision.
  The observer card is that loop reduced to one card looking at itself.
- **[[level-crossing]].** The step in which something at one level of
  description is treated as belonging to another. The experiment succeeds
  only if one can be exhibited.
- **[[quine]].** A program that prints its own source and says nothing about
  it. It is the baseline the observer card has to exceed.
- **[[render-integrate-execute]].** Rendering a result, integrating it and
  executing cells in it, each as a separate decision. The observer card's
  boundaries are a special case.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[observer-card-experiment]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[observer-card-experiment]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Each card's Queries section in this vault is a small observer: the card
retrieves itself by name and reports its own links.

